    { href: '/maintenance/generator-maintenance-checklist/', title: 'Maintenance checklist', description: 'Follow the equipment’s required service schedule.', category: 'Maintenance' },
  ]}
>
  <section id="at-a-glance">
    <h2>Whole house generator installation at a glance</h2>
    <p>The exact sequence and responsible trade vary by jurisdiction, contractor and equipment. Use this table to make sure the written proposal identifies who owns each step rather than assuming it is included.</p>
    <div class="not-prose mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-[#d9e0ea]">
      <table class="mobile-card-table sm:min-w-[820px] w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm">
        <caption class="sr-only">Typical whole house generator installation stages, responsible parties, and homeowner verification questions</caption>
        <thead class="bg-[#edf3fb] text-primary"><tr><th scope="col" class="p-3">Stage</th><th scope="col" class="p-3">Typical lead</th><th scope="col" class="p-3">What the homeowner should verify</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 text-gray-800">
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3 font-semibold">Load & site assessment</th><td class="p-3">Qualified installer / electrical professional</td><td class="p-3">Backed-up loads, motor starts, exact model/fuel, panel, fuel capacity, proposed location and site constraints.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3 font-semibold">Design & written scope</th><td class="p-3">Installer with required licensed trades</td><td class="p-3">Generator and ATS model numbers, load-management plan, electrical/fuel routes, exclusions, allowances and service responsibilities.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3 font-semibold">Permits & approvals</th><td class="p-3">Contractor and/or homeowner, depending on local rules</td><td class="p-3">Who applies, required inspections, utility coordination, HOA/property approvals and any conditions before work starts.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3 font-semibold">Pad / site preparation</th><td class="p-3">Installer or site contractor</td><td class="p-3">Manufacturer-approved base, drainage, access, clearances, exhaust direction, service space and restoration scope.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3 font-semibold">Fuel work</th><td class="p-3">Qualified gas / propane professional as required</td><td class="p-3">Fuel type, capacity, pressure/flow, pipe route and size, meter/regulator or tank work, testing and permit scope.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3 font-semibold">Electrical & transfer equipment</th><td class="p-3">Qualified electrical professional</td><td class="p-3">ATS/service arrangement, backed-up circuits, load management, wiring/conduit route, panel work and required shutdowns.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3 font-semibold">Inspection & commissioning</th><td class="p-3">Authority having jurisdiction + installer</td><td class="p-3">Required inspection status, startup/transfer test, alarms, operating limits, final configuration and unresolved punch-list items.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="p-3 font-semibold">Owner handoff</th><td class="p-3">Installer and homeowner</td><td class="p-3">Manuals, model/serial records, warranty registration, maintenance schedule, service contact and safe operating instructions.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>For the related budget and equipment decisions, see the <a href="/cost/installation-cost-breakdown/">whole house generator installation cost breakdown</a>, <a href="/installation/generator-pad-and-placement/">pad and placement guide</a>, <a href="/installation/automatic-transfer-switch-guide/">automatic transfer switch guide</a>, <a href="/installation/permits-and-local-requirements/">permit and local-requirements guide</a>, and <a href="/planning/quotes/">three-quote comparison tool</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="assessment">
    <h2>1. Whole house generator site and load assessment</h2>
    <p><strong>Whole house generator installation should start with a site-specific load and property assessment.</strong> A qualified local professional should review which loads the homeowner expects to back up, the exact generator/fuel options, the electrical service and panel, available fuel, potential equipment locations, and applicable local requirements. Bring photographs and model numbers for HVAC, pumps, and other large loads. Square footage alone is not enough information for a project design.</p>
    <p>The professional should confirm the manufacturer’s current installation manual and the fuel and electrical conditions required for the selected equipment. This publication has not surveyed a property or produced an electrical load calculation.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="proposal">
    <h2>2. Ask for a complete written proposal</h2>
    <p>Before accepting a proposal, check that it identifies exact equipment and makes the installation boundaries clear:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Generator model, fuel, rating, transfer switch/ATS model, panel/service arrangement, and any load-management accessories.</li>
      <li>Which circuits are included, which loads may be shed, and how the system is expected to operate.</li>
      <li>Fuel and electrical connection scope, routes, meter/regulator or panel work, and any allowances.</li>
      <li>Site preparation/base, restoration, permits, inspections, utility coordination, commissioning, and owner orientation.</li>
      <li>Written exclusions, warranty responsibility, future service provider, schedule, payment milestones, and change-order process.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Generac describes its own dealer consultation as covering placement, installation-site requirements, system requirements, fuel source/connection, and property restrictions; exact scope varies by manufacturer and project.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="approvals">
    <h2>3. Confirm permits and approvals</h2>
    <p>Permit types and required approvals vary with the property, equipment, utility, and jurisdiction. Ask the contractor which permits and inspections are required, who obtains them, which edition/local amendments apply, and whether HOA/property approvals or utility coordination are needed.</p>
    <p>Do not accept “no permit is needed” without confirmation from the relevant local authority. Do not start electrical or fuel-system work until the required approvals are in place.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="preparation">
    <h2>4. Site preparation, equipment setting, and connections</h2>
    <p>The exact installation sequence depends on the equipment and site. The work may include preparing an approved mounting base, placing the generator, routing fuel and electrical connections, installing the specified transfer equipment, and completing required inspections. A professional must follow the model’s current manual for clearances, exhaust, ventilation, access, fuel pressure/flow, wiring, and mounting.</p>
    <figure class="not-prose my-6 rounded-xl border border-[#d9e0ea] bg-[#f7f9fc] p-4 sm:p-6">
